<%# Body tab of the Bodies & Food page %>
<p class="page-subtitle">From trunk to tusks to toes, elephants have amazing bodies. Let's take a closer look!</p>

<div class="section">
  <h2>How big are elephants?</h2>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Kind of elephant</th>
        <th>How tall (grown-up male, to the shoulder)</th>
        <th>How heavy (grown-up male)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>African savanna elephant</strong></td>
        <td>3–4 m (10–13 ft)</td>
        <td>4,500–6,800 kg</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>African forest elephant</strong></td>
        <td>2.2–2.5 m (7–8 ft)</td>
        <td>2,000–4,000 kg</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Asian elephant</strong></td>
        <td>2.5–3 m (8–10 ft)</td>
        <td>3,500–5,000 kg</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The biggest savanna elephants are taller than a basketball hoop! Females are smaller than males. A baby elephant,
    called a <em>calf</em>, weighs about 100 kg when it's born. That's more than a grown-up person!
  </p>

  <h3>Size comparison</h3>
  <p>Here's how the three kinds of elephant compare to a person (1.8 m / 6 ft) and a car (1.5 m / 5 ft tall).</p>
  <div style="background: var(--surface, #faf6ee); border: 1px solid var(--border, #e0dbd0); border-radius: 16px; padding: 1.5rem; overflow-x: auto; box-shadow: 0 2px 12px rgba(0,0,0,0.06);">
